WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.690
Physical methods can separate mixture
components because they exploit

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:06.947
differences such as solubility,
boiling behavior, or magnetism.

00:00:07.313 --> 00:00:11.482
Separating a compound into simpler
substances requires a chemical change.

00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:15.665
Seawater is a mixture because its
dissolved salt amount can vary,

00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:19.611
and air has several gases even
when I cannot see separate layers.

00:00:20.403 --> 00:00:20.928
Yes.

00:00:21.141 --> 00:00:22.920
Many alloys are mixtures too.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:27.502
Brass contains copper and zinc,
steel contains iron with carbon

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.878
and often other elements.

00:00:29.258 --> 00:00:31.996
The proportions and structure
affect their properties.

00:00:32.294 --> 00:00:34.389
They are not simply one new element.

00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:40.247
Steel reminds me that an alloy can include
a non-metal because carbon is a non-metal.

00:00:41.065 --> 00:00:41.842
Exactly.

00:00:42.073 --> 00:00:45.107
At this level, we classify
familiar alloys as mixtures.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.383
Real materials can have more complicated
phases, but the useful question

00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:54.383
remains: one element, one compound,
or more than one substance together?

00:00:54.725 --> 00:00:56.981
A carton says pure orange juice.

00:00:57.561 --> 00:00:59.494
Does pure mean the same
thing in chemistry?

00:01:00.256 --> 00:01:01.211
Surely it means only

00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:02.158
one substance,

00:01:02.706 --> 00:01:03.071
or have

00:01:03.071 --> 00:01:03.168
I

00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:04.526
fallen into the fruit trap?

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:06.207
The fruit trap.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:09.955
Juice contains water,
sugars, and other substances.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.536
In chemistry, a pure substance
is a single element or compound

00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:16.384
without other substances mixed in.

00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:20.978
Pure water contains just water,
not dissolved salt or juice.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.736
How can a chemist check
rather than trust the label?

00:01:25.568 --> 00:01:28.913
A pure sample often has a
characteristic sharp melting

00:01:28.913 --> 00:01:30.511
point at a specified pressure.

00:01:31.178 --> 00:01:34.790
Many soluble impurities lower
and broaden the melting range.

00:01:35.274 --> 00:01:39.114
Comparing a measured value with a
reference can provide evidence of purity.

00:01:39.835 --> 00:01:45.278
Pure ice melts at zero degrees
Celsius and water boils at 100, but

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:46.950
do I need to mention conditions?

00:01:47.658 --> 00:01:48.237
Yes.

00:01:48.555 --> 00:01:52.337
Those familiar values are approximately
for ordinary atmospheric pressure.

00:01:52.793 --> 00:01:55.643
Change the pressure and
boiling temperature changes.

00:01:56.044 --> 00:01:59.839
Impurities can also change boiling
behavior, but not every mixture

00:01:59.839 --> 00:02:01.940
follows a single universal rule.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.872
Dissolving salt in water raises its
boiling point under comparable conditions,

00:02:07.284 --> 00:02:11.958
but I should not say every impurity always
raises every liquid's boiling point.

00:02:12.753 --> 00:02:13.540
Exactly.

00:02:13.874 --> 00:02:18.672
Salt also lowers water's freezing point,
which helps explain gritting icy roads.

00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:23.302
A sharp melting point is useful
evidence, not a magical guarantee that

00:02:23.338 --> 00:02:25.418
every possible impurity has vanished.

00:02:26.235 --> 00:02:29.482
Careful evidence beats an
impressive-sounding always.

00:02:29.903 --> 00:02:32.499
My answer can be accurate
without being enormous

00:02:32.882 --> 00:02:36.946
I dissolve 10 grams of sugar
in 100 grams of water without

00:02:36.946 --> 00:02:38.991
spilling or evaporating anything.

00:02:39.449 --> 00:02:41.148
The crystals disappear from sight.

00:02:41.815 --> 00:02:43.114
What mass should remain?

00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:46.518
100 grams because the sugar vanished.

00:02:47.043 --> 00:02:49.760
Wait, that would be a
disappearing matter trick.

00:02:50.522 --> 00:02:51.300
Exactly.

00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:53.949
The total is 110 grams.

00:02:54.364 --> 00:02:56.707
Sugar particles spread
throughout the water.

00:02:57.016 --> 00:02:58.329
They are not destroyed.

00:02:58.731 --> 00:03:02.123
The sugar is the solute, the
water the solvent, and the

00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:03.873
resulting mixture a solution.

00:03:04.573 --> 00:03:09.937
Solute dissolves, solvent does the
dissolving, solution is the mixture, and

00:03:09.937 --> 00:03:12.343
clear does not necessarily mean colorless.

00:03:13.031 --> 00:03:13.459
Good.

00:03:13.777 --> 00:03:16.145
Some solutions are
colored but transparent.

00:03:16.485 --> 00:03:20.331
A substance is soluble or
insoluble in a particular solvent

00:03:20.372 --> 00:03:21.866
under particular conditions.

00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:25.273
Water dissolves many
substances, but not everything.

00:03:25.693 --> 00:03:28.696
Oil does not simply dissolve
because water is present.

00:03:29.449 --> 00:03:32.410
Are concentrated and
saturated the same word?

00:03:33.226 --> 00:03:33.591
No.

00:03:34.175 --> 00:03:37.654
Concentration describes an amount
of solute per volume of solution.

00:03:37.939 --> 00:03:39.647
For example, grams per liter.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:42.983
Saturated means the solution
has reached its solubility

00:03:42.983 --> 00:03:44.499
limit under those conditions.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.337
Concentrated does not
automatically mean saturated.

00:03:49.114 --> 00:03:52.335
Adding water makes the same
solute amount more spread out,

00:03:52.556 --> 00:03:54.303
so concentration decreases.

00:03:55.102 --> 00:03:55.692
Yes.

00:03:56.029 --> 00:03:59.521
Adding dissolved solute or removing
some solvent can increase it.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:03.868
Solubility is the maximum amount that
can dissolve in a specified quantity

00:04:03.871 --> 00:04:05.740
of solvent at a stated temperature.

00:04:06.499 --> 00:04:09.932
Stirring faster must mean more
sugar can dissolve forever.

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:15.123
Stirring usually helps it dissolve faster,
but does not remove the saturation limit.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:19.915
Heating increases the solubility of
many solids, such as sugar, but not

00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:21.563
every substance behaves that way.

00:04:21.939 --> 00:04:25.124
Gases often become less
soluble as temperature rises.

00:04:25.911 --> 00:04:32.206
How fast, how concentrated, and how much
can dissolve are three different questions

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:35.830
Our mystery beaker contains
sand, salt, and water.

00:04:36.449 --> 00:04:39.157
Can filter paper remove
both solids from the liquid?

00:04:39.927 --> 00:04:40.000
Sand gets trapped, but dissolved
salt passes through with the water.
